\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Heat kernels as compact gauge transfer weights}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{G} be a compact connected Lie group, equipped with a
positive Ad-invariant inner product on its real Lie algebra.
Let #{\Delta_G\le0} be the Laplace operator for the resulting
bi-invariant metric. For #{\tau>0}, let #{k_\tau} be the heat
kernel started at the identity, as a density against Haar
probability #{\mu_G}, with convention
##{\partial_\tau k_\tau=\tfrac12\Delta_G k_\tau.}
For each complex unitary irreducible #{\rho}, let #{c_\rho\ge0}
be the eigenvalue of #{-\Delta_G} on its matrix coefficients.
Then #{k_\tau} satisfies all the kernel hypotheses in the
\vocabk{spatial transfer data}{fdip-007Z}, with
##{a_\rho(\tau)=e^{-\tau c_\rho /2},\qquad
 k_\tau(g)=\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}
 d_\rho a_\rho(\tau)\chi_\rho(g).}
The series is uniformly absolutely convergent, and
##{\sum_\rho d_\rho^2a_\rho(\tau)=k_\tau(e)<\infty.}
In particular #{k_\tau(g)>0} for every #{g}, and
#{a_{\mathrm{triv}}(\tau)=1}.}

\proof{
\p{The Brownian motion generated by #{\Delta_G/2} has zero
drift and no jumps. Its positive-time density is smooth and
hence in #{L^2(G,\mu_G)} by compactness. These are the elliptic
hypotheses discussed in \citet{p. 139}{levy2010holonomy}.
The Ad-invariant metric makes conjugations isometries fixing
#{e}, so uniqueness of the heat kernel makes #{k_\tau} central.
Inversion is also such an isometry for a bi-invariant metric,
which gives #{k_\tau(g^{-1})=k_\tau(g)}. The density is real.
Lévy's Proposition 4.2.3 therefore applies: the group is
connected, the process is conjugation invariant, and its
density belongs to #{L^2} at every positive time. It gives
strict positivity everywhere
\citet{pp. 137–138}{levy2010holonomy}.}

\p{In Lévy's spectral notation the Brownian exponent is
#{\lambda_\rho=c_\rho /2}. Equations (53)–(55) give the displayed
expansion, its summability and uniform absolute convergence
\citet{pp. 136–137}{levy2010holonomy}. That source uses conjugate
characters. Reindexing by the dual representation gives the
formula here, since #{\chi_{\rho^*}=\overline{\chi_\rho}},
#{d_{\rho^*}=d_\rho} and #{c_{\rho^*}=c_\rho}; the last equality
follows because the real Laplacian commutes with complex
conjugation. Equivalently, the heat equation acts on each
Peter–Weyl coefficient space by #{e^{-\tau c_\rho /2}};
this scalar is #{a_\rho}, while its character coefficient is
#{d_\rho a_\rho}. Evaluation at #{e}, where
#{\chi_\rho(e)=d_\rho}, proves the stated sum. Every exponential
is strictly positive, and the trivial representation has
#{c_{\mathrm{triv}}=0}.}

\p{To match the reference measure, let #{v_G} be the total
Riemannian volume of the chosen metric and let #{h_\tau} be
the density against that volume. Since
#{d\mu_G=d\mathrm{vol}_G/v_G}, the Haar-probability density
is #{k_\tau=v_Gh_\tau}. Thus Lévy's unit-volume convention
\citet{§3.1, p. 107}{levy2010holonomy} is implemented by
changing the density, without rescaling the metric,
#{\Delta_G} or #{c_\rho}. This proves the exact normalized-Haar
form required by the [spatial transfer data](fdip-007Z).}
}

\p{Connectedness is used for strict pointwise positivity:
on a disconnected Lie group, Brownian motion started at #{e}
stays in the identity component. The general transfer
definition still includes disconnected groups with other
kernels satisfying its hypotheses.}
